  "body": "## Change and mathematical scope\n\nConstruction: realize `y -> y \u00b1 (x+1) mod Q` for every positive, canonical binary-encoded `P,Q,G,S` and arbitrary Boolean payload of volume `V=P Q\u00b2 G S`. The two fixed programs use fourteen distinct tapes, seven symbols, and 552 instructions each. They preserve source/descriptors and return their heads, create a separate owned output, and erase all scratch cells and markers. Output and scratch roles must initially be blank.\n\nThe complete cost is **449V local transitions and 41V visited cells**, including setup, copying, marker handling, restoration, and erasure. For every valid input, the exact transition count is the PR2776 zero-carry count plus seven. The all-size proof explains the changed split invariant and path count, with the predecessor counter lemmas retained explicitly. This completes the coefficient/carry pairs `(+1,+1)` and `(-1,-1)` left open by PR2776; it does not discharge H2, implement a complete recursive caller, or claim a new \u03ba.\n\n## Task and overlap\n\nBase: `e24ccc79caa1943fb126c61b45032fefddf2d3f2`. Scientific predecessor: [PR2776](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/pull/2776), pinned to `7de16285a02ebb45cf46e7501baa7eafa78335e3`. Its first open question requests a named nonzero-carry kernel. This package supplies that narrower component while retaining attribution and the exact predecessor exports.\n\nScope is **refined** from `uniform-transfer-audit` to the user-assigned bit-supplier track, OS seed 14. TASK.json includes the original card, source pins, stopping rule, and overlap coverage. The originating task is `01a1335e-7a24-73c3-a549-9bcb5496a363`. No pinned coordination issue exists; the narrow claim and its result remain in the local research capture. The last refresh covers 692 open PRs and 1474 discussion records, plus local package metadata and the ledger; it does not certify novelty across unpublished work or arbitrary diffs.\n\n## Validation\n\nRun from the repository root:\n\n```sh\npython3.15 -I -B research/fixed-tape-unit-carry/verify.py\npython3.15 -I -B -O research/fixed-tape-unit-carry/test_integrity.py\n```\n\nExpected: `PASS_FIXED_TAPE_UNIT_CARRY` and `PASS_UNIT_CARRY_INTEGRITY`.\n\nPrimary research uses stable CPython 3.15.0 through the Math research-python wrapper. The generic repository runner explicitly pins Python 3.12; a separate CPython 3.12.12 compatibility replay also passes:\n\n```sh\npython3.12 -B scripts/research_package.py --package research/fixed-tape-unit-carry\n```\n\nThe independent output-coordinate gather model shares no split traversal, counters, or assembler with the producer. A separate dense interpreter checks the serialized machine. Evidence includes 270 dense executions over 78 shapes, 24 sparse/dense whole-bank comparisons, 156 same-input predecessor comparisons, 12 nontrivial inverse round trips, and 12 actual nonzero-carry source specializations. Ten semantic controls reject wrong endpoints, missing resets/restoration, retained scratch, payload corruption, nonlocal movement, and an endpoint-correct machine with two unbilled moves. Eight separate integrity/export controls also pass, including optimized read-only replay without Git.\n\nFinite checks corroborate the implementation; the written invariant and cost argument establish the claimed all-size scope with explicit inherited lemmas. There is no independent human or proof-assistant review, no full selected payload replay, and no claim that local checks establish hosted CI. Global historical suites were not rerun because this additive package changes no selected construction or runner.\n\n## Credit\n\nDavid Arias supplies PR2776's machine, assembler, counter converter, split/merge macros, cleanup, proof, and separate dense interpreter. Jose Manuel supplies the retained selected q-adic lowering and its ordered-affine lineage. Frozen source bytes and Apache-2.0 licensing remain intact. New work was prepared for Charles Bethin with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance in derivation, implementation, verification, and writing.\n\n## Open next questions\n\nThe package README and TASK.json give two bounded follow-ups: construct one different fixed carry with a complete stopping invariant, or bind this kernel to one actual caller's role ownership and lifetimes. Both require further physical cost accounting; this kernel alone does not settle the complete supplier.\n",
